now that we're in 2021 apple's m1 max  have proven to be  incredibly powerful for the price but  what happens when we put  the absolute least expensive macbook  the 999 dollar base m1 macbook air up  against  the absolute most expensive macbook  the 3899 16 inch macbook pro  with the 8 core i9 2.4 gig processor  and the insane 5600 am graphics chip  which by the way was a major improvement  over the previous graphics options now  this comparison  is just a bit insane because the 16 inch  is basically  four times more expensive it's a much  larger machine in general and it has  two cooling fans well the macbook air  has  zero but what if the air  could actually compete in some areas if  it can  it'll prove just how good apple silicon  really is  and it'll give us a sneak peek at the  potential performance  of the upcoming 14 and 16 inch m1x  macbook pros  which are expected to boost the market  share of apple silicon max  all the way up to 7 so let's get right  into the performance testing  with geekbench 5. in terms of single  core cpu performance  the m1 air is way ahead because it uses  only four  very powerful and efficient performance  cores  and making it even more surprising the  m1 air  actually outperforms the 16-inch in  terms of multi-core performance as well  now if we go over to real world  performance using the 10 minute  cinebench r23 stress test  the 16 inch now takes over by a huge 38  percent  because the air has no fans at all so it  starts to throttle down the performance  so in terms of cpu power the air is  better  for short bursts of performance while  the 16 inch is better for  longer and heavier workloads which we'll  get into in just a second  but now i want to move on to graphics  performance and this is where the  16 inch pro's 5600 m gpu  really shines being over twice as fast  as the base 7 core gpu on the m1 air  in terms of geekbench 5 metal  performance this is going to impact  everything from  gaming to video editing performance but  we've actually estimated  that the future m1x 16-inch pro with the  integrated  16 core gpu is gonna beat the current  dedicated 5600 m graphics so that's very  exciting  now moving on to gaming performance we  tested gfx bench 5.0  using the aztec runes off-screen test  and here  there isn't as big of a difference but  the 16-inch is still  95 more powerful than the air which is a  big deal for gaming  we also tested games like league of  legends where the m1 air was able to  maintain  an average of 82 frames per second but  it's still  nothing compared to the 16 inch and then  in fortnight  the air had less than 60 fps average so  it couldn't keep up with the 16 inch pro  which got very good frame rates now on  top of that you have to use workarounds  like crossover and parallel 16 to run  windows games on the m1 and a lot of  them won't even run  while the 16 inch can easily use boot  camp which performs  much better and more reliably so it's  very clear which one is better for  gaming  now since the m1x 16 inch is expected to  have more graphics power  than the 5600m we should actually get  very great aaa gaming results later this  year  and hopefully a lot of the bugs in  crossover and other apps will be fixed  by then  i personally think that a lot of people  will eventually start to prefer  macs over windows laptops for gaming  so it's going to be very interesting to  see what's going to happen over the next  couple of years  moving on let's focus on a variety of  common tasks for mac users  like photo editing xcode programming and  logic pro music production  so let's start with the latter we tested  the new logic pro benchmark  which measures how many tracks the  machine can run without overloading the  cpu  and surprisingly the macbook air was  right behind the 16-inch pro  which nobody was expecting because of  the huge difference  in raw multi-core performance so for  people who are producing music  the new m1 air is actually a killer  value  and the upcoming m1x max are gonna be  just insane now moving on to xcode  programming  this is where i was absolutely blown  away  the m1 macbook air destroyed the 16-inch  pro  being a massive 80 faster  which doesn't make any sense if you look  at the previous results  and it even did it without a fan while  the 16 inch was loud and very hot  i personally think that this is because  of the more efficient and optimized  arm-based  architecture including the unified  memory  even though it only has 8 gigs of ram  compared to  32 gigs on the 16 inch so the future is  looking  very bright for programmers now let's  get into photo editing with lightroom  classic  which by the way hasn't been updated for  apple silicon  just yet so it's using rosetta to run  we exported 50 raw 42 megapixel photos  and the air is performing surprisingly  well  being only 38 seconds slower than the  16-inch pro  even though it has 4 times less ram  however  when we bump up this test to 500 raw  photos  instead of just 50 the macbook air  begins to suffer  from thermal throttling because of the  lack of a fan  and the difference becomes much bigger  with the 16 inch being around 8 minutes  faster but in terms of editing  smoothness the  m1 is still very quick and snappy  because of the arm chip  so it really only makes a difference  when exporting photos  so if you work with very large projects  the 16 inch is still the way to go now  let's finally get into video editing  with final cut pro  starting with the bruce x benchmark  which fully relies on graphics power  and the 6-inch pro was three times  faster  which is extremely impressive then  moving on to exporting a 5-minute 4k  h.264 clip which is the most common  format that most youtubers use  the 16 inch was only 3 seconds faster  than the air  and that's because this test is mostly  limited by the hardware encoders  and the ones on the m1 are faster next  up we have a couple of stabilization  tests  starting with stabilizing a 1 minute  hevc clip  and surprisingly they both finished in  just 10 seconds  once again limited by the encoders but  now when stabilizing c200  canon raw footage the 16 inch is quite a  bit ahead  because there are no encoders for this  type of footage  so it depends on a mixture of cpu and  gpu performance  moving on i decided to test some very  heavy footage  exporting a 5 minute 8k redraw clip  and here the difference is massive with  the 16 inch being over  three times as fast because of the extra  graphics performance  and the fact that it actually has fans  so if you're doing  any kind of heavy video editing the 16  inch is currently  the better option for sure now i want to  finish off with  one test which was heavily requested  transcoding and exporting  the new canon r5 10-bit 422 footage  surprisingly the macbook air absolutely  destroyed the 16-inch  in terms of the 5-minute export test and  that's because  the m1 max are currently the only  computers in the world  that have hardware encoders and decoders  for this new codec now if you choose to  transcode this footage  instead the massive difference remains  with the m1 air only taking  13 minutes to transcode a 30 minute clip  compared to 40 minutes for the 16 inch  macbook pro  which is just insane so with all of that  testing out of the way  i want to remind you guys that this is  the absolute  weakest apple silicon macbook that will  ever be made  compared to the absolute most powerful  intel macbook  ever made you can quite literally buy  four of these macbook airs  for the same price of this 16-inch pro  and amazingly when it comes to  real-world performance  the air isn't as far off as you'd expect  it to be and there's absolutely  no fan noise at all and in some of these  tests like  xcode programming and canon r5 video  editing  the air was actually faster and one  thing to keep in mind  is that the m1 chip uses much less power  only 13 watts for the cpu compared to  60 on the 16 inch and 5.6 watts for the  